# Grep Check Registry — postgres pack fragment
#
# SQL / SQLAlchemy / Postgres-migration checks. The Phase 3
# installer concatenates this fragment with core/companion/
# checks.yml.fragment (and any other selected pack fragments)
# into the project-side .claude/checks.yml. Path globs use
# placeholder vocabulary that the installer substitutes per
# project from convention_map.md.
#
# Helper names referenced in patterns (e.g., _latest_obs_sql,
# writer_engine, admin_engine, reader_engine) and table names
# (users, subscriptions, payments) are placeholders for the
# equivalent identifiers in the consuming project's
# convention_map.md / migrations. Override pattern strings
# post-install if your project uses different names.
#
# Format docs: WORKFLOW.md §6.5

checks:
  # ── Database role / GRANT defense-in-depth ─────────────────────

  - id: grant-sensitive-table
    name: "GRANT on sensitive PII table to non-admin role"
    category: security
    severity: critical
    paths: ["<migrations dir>/"]
    include: ["*.sql"]
    pattern: '^\s*GRANT\s+.*\s+ON\s+.*\b(users|subscriptions|stripe_events|payments|stripe_subscriptions)\b.*\s+TO\s+app_'
    notes: |
      The table name is matched ANYWHERE between `ON` and `TO app_`, not
      immediately after `ON`. The tighter form missed two ordinary spellings —
      `GRANT SELECT ON public.users TO app_reader` (schema-qualified) and
      `GRANT SELECT ON users, orders TO app_reader` (multi-table) — on a
      blocking, critical gate. `ON TABLE users` is a third. Measured on a
      16-case labelled matrix: the tight form was wrong on 6, this one on 0;
      the sanctioned remediations (`v_public_users`, `users_archive`,
      `payments_summary`) all stay quiet, because `_` is a word character and
      the `\b` holds.

      What `.*` costs, named rather than discovered later: two low-frequency
      false positives. A line carrying TWO grants where only the first names a
      listed table (`GRANT ... ON users TO admin_role; GRANT ... ON orders TO
      app_reader;`), and a listed table appearing in a comment between `ON` and
      `TO` (`GRANT SELECT ON orders /* not users */ TO app_reader;`). Both are
      waivable with `-- no-check: grant-sensitive-table`.

      What it does NOT close — four gaps, not one, because a note whose stated
      job is naming the gaps has to name them:
        1. a GRANT wrapped across lines (`GRANT SELECT (id, email)` /
           `ON users TO app_reader;`) — invisible to ANY table alternation,
           since the line holding the table holds no `GRANT`;
        2. `GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO app_reader;` — a real
           grant of every sensitive table, naming none of them;
        3. lowercase `grant select on users to app_reader;`, which is ordinary
           in migrations and which the matrix above does not cover;
        4. a table named only via a view or synonym.
      (1) and (2) are NARROWED — not closed — as of Phase 217 by the two sibling
      checks immediately below, `grant-wrapped-continuation` and
      `grant-all-tables-in-schema`, backported from the extraction source
      (`Q-262`). Neither is an alternation branch here on purpose: no table
      alternation can match a line that contains no table, which is the whole
      shape of gap (1). **An earlier draft of this paragraph said "CLOSED", and
      that was wrong in a way a reader would have relied on** — the review round
      demonstrated it. What each sibling still misses is stated in its own
      `notes:`; read those before treating either gap as gone. The residual that
      matters most here: a THREE-line wrap (`GRANT SELECT` / `ON users` /
      `TO app_reader;`) puts `TO app_role` on its own line, so the continuation
      sibling — which requires `ON … TO app_` together — matches nothing.
      (3) is a one-word fix (`grep -i`) that is still NOT taken. It is now
      *measured* rather than merely unmeasured — 0 lowercase `grant` statements
      across the 191 `.sql` files of the one real corpus — but 0 incidence is
      evidence of 0 cost, not evidence of need, and it widens a blocking
      critical gate. Filed rather than shipped.
      (4) is out of reach of any grep and stays open.

      The table list is deliberately short and is NOT a generic set — see
      `security_map.md` Check A01 §2. (NOT the convention map: it contains no
      occurrence of `grant`, and an earlier draft of this note sent readers
      there.) It is inherited from the extraction source and is the
      one part of this rule a consumer is expected to replace.
    description: "GRANT on sensitive PII table targeting app_reader/app_writer — erodes the DB role defense-in-depth. Use a dedicated view (v_public_*) or keep the access on admin_engine."
    convention: "GRANTs on sensitive tables"
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: true

  # Sibling 1 of grant-sensitive-table, closing gap (2) above: the one GRANT
  # spelling that names no table at all and therefore cannot be caught by any
  # table alternation. `GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO
  # app_reader` grants on every sensitive table at once, present and future.
  #
  # This is a SHAPE rule, not a table rule, and that is deliberate: it names no
  # table, so it carries none of the drift risk that made the sibling's
  # hand-copied five-name list rot.
  - id: grant-all-tables-in-schema
    name: "GRANT on ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA to non-admin role"
    category: security
    severity: critical
    paths: ["<migrations dir>/"]
    include: ["*.sql"]
    pattern: '^\s*GRANT\s+.*\s+ON\s+ALL\s+TABLES\s+IN\s+SCHEMA\s+\w+\s+TO\s+app_'
    description: "GRANT ... ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA ... TO app_reader/app_writer — a blanket grant that covers every table existing in that schema at execution time, including every sensitive one, and names none of them so the grant-sensitive-table alternation cannot see it. Grant per-table, or use a dedicated view (v_public_*), or keep the access on the admin engine. NOTE it does NOT cover tables created later — that is ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES, which no check here matches."
    convention: "GRANTs on sensitive tables"
    notes: "Backported in Phase 217 (Q-262) from the extraction source, where it was authored as the highest-value bypass of the widened sibling. Ships blocking with an EMPTY baseline: measured 0 hits across the 191 `.sql` files of the one real corpus, so adoption costs nothing there. The `app_` role prefix matches the sibling's existing convention in this same fragment — it is the pack's assumed non-admin role naming, and a consumer using other role names must localize both checks together. WHAT IT DOES NOT MATCH, named because a blocking critical check that overstates its reach is worse than one that admits a gap (all four demonstrated by the Phase 217 review round): a quoted schema identifier (`IN SCHEMA \"public\"` — `\\w+` cannot match the quotes); a multi-schema grant (`IN SCHEMA public, analytics`); `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES ... GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO app_reader`, which is the actual future-tables mechanism and is matched by nothing in this pack; and, like every check here and like its pre-existing sibling grant-sensitive-table, a statement inside a `/* */` block comment, since the runner is line-oriented and has no comment awareness. A commented-out rollback plan therefore fires this gate; waive it with `-- no-check: grant-all-tables-in-schema` on the reported line."
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: true

  # Sibling 2, closing gap (1) above. The runner is LINE-based, so a GRANT whose
  # `ON <table> TO app_role` clause is wrapped onto a continuation line matches
  # neither half of the sibling's pattern: the GRANT line has no `ON ... TO
  # app_`, and the continuation line has no `GRANT`. Matching on the
  # continuation line rather than with a multi-line pattern is forced by the
  # runner being line-based; it is also the narrower net, since a bare leading
  # `ON ... TO app_` in a .sql file has no other meaning.
  - id: grant-wrapped-continuation
    name: "GRANT with its ON ... TO app_role clause wrapped onto a continuation line"
    category: security
    severity: critical
    paths: ["<migrations dir>/"]
    include: ["*.sql"]
    pattern: '^\s*ON\s+.*\bTO\s+app_'
    description: "A GRANT statement whose `ON <table> TO app_reader/app_writer` clause is wrapped onto its own line. The grant-sensitive-table gate is a line-based grep and cannot see either half, so the statement passes a blocking CRITICAL PII check silently regardless of which table it names. Reflow the statement so `ON <table> TO app_role` sits on the GRANT line; long column lists stay on that line too."
    convention: "GRANTs on sensitive tables"
    notes: "Backported in Phase 217 (Q-262). Ships blocking with an EMPTY baseline: measured 0 hits across the 191 `.sql` files of the one real corpus. In the extraction source both pre-existing occurrences were reflowed rather than baselined, which is the intended remediation — reflowing also moves the grant into the grant-sensitive-table gate for the first time, so expect that check to fire on a reflow and to need a deliberate, documented baseline entry if the grant is sanctioned. Same `app_` role-prefix caveat as its sibling. WHAT IT DOES NOT MATCH: a THREE-line wrap, where `TO app_role` sits on its own line — this pattern needs `ON` and `TO app_` on the SAME line, so `GRANT SELECT` / `ON users` / `TO app_reader;` passes every check in this pack. That is the surviving half of gap (1) above. An earlier draft of this note claimed a bare leading `ON ... TO app_` in a .sql file \"has no other meaning\"; inside a `/* */` block comment it has no SQL meaning at all, and the line-oriented runner cannot tell, so a commented-out rollback plan fires this blocking critical gate — the same property its pre-existing sibling grant-sensitive-table has. Waive with `-- no-check: grant-wrapped-continuation` on the reported line."
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: true

  # ── SQL safety / engine selection ──────────────────────────────

  - id: raw-row-number
    name: "Bypassed Helpers — Raw ROW_NUMBER()"
    category: correctness
    severity: high
    paths: ["<api module>/"]
    include: ["*.py"]
    exclude: ["*helpers.py"]
    pattern: 'ROW_NUMBER\(\)'
    description: "Raw ROW_NUMBER() — verify if vintage dedup pattern; if so, use _latest_obs_sql() helper"
    convention: "Vintage deduplication helper"
    notes: "Needs LLM triage: non-vintage uses of ROW_NUMBER() (e.g., ranking by date) are false positives"
    used_by: [codebase-review]

  - id: sql-fstring
    name: "f-string SQL"
    category: correctness
    severity: critical
    paths: ["<api module>/", "<scripts dir>/"]
    include: ["*.py"]
    exclude: ["*test*"]
    pattern: '([fF][rR]?|[rR][fF])["'']{1,3}(SELECT|INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE|ALTER|DROP)\b'
    description: "f-string SQL detected — use parameterized queries"
    convention: "SQL safety"
    notes: "Quote-agnostic by design: the single-quoted form is what you reach for when the statement embeds \"quoted identifiers\", which is the injection-shaped case. Do NOT add -i (matches English prose: f\"Deleted {n}...\") and do NOT require a same-line FROM/WHERE/JOIN/SET (it narrows, dropping f\"SELECT 1\" and the first line of a multi-line f-string). Phase 212 widened the prefix and quote-count arms and added the trailing \\b; all three were measured by set comparison over 27,830 .py files in four real corpora — zero hits lost. WHAT IT STILL CANNOT SEE, stated because a critical check that overstates its reach is worse than one that admits a gap: grep is line-oriented, so a triple-quoted f-string whose keyword falls on the NEXT line (sql = f\"\"\"\\n    SELECT ...) is invisible here, and a leading space inside the quotes (f\"\"\" SELECT) is deliberately not matched because allowing it produced a false positive on print(f\"  INSERT {x}\") and bought no real hit. That residual is CLOSED as of Phase 217 (Q-244) by semgrep-sql-fstring-multiline, a sibling rule in the same YAML that matches the opening triple quote and the keyword on the following line — semgrep's regex engine is multi-line where this runner is not. It covers the shape regardless of destination, which matters because the two real production sites found in the extraction source are NOT the same shape: one binds the f-string to a variable, the other returns it directly, and the filing described only the first. Verified against that corpus: exactly those 2 sites and no others, with 0 false positives across every non-test module in scope."
    used_by: [codebase-review]

  - id: wrong-engine
    name: "Wrong Engine for Reads"
    category: correctness
    severity: high
    paths: ["<api module>/routes/", "<api module>/tools/"]
    include: ["*.py"]
    pattern: '(admin_engine|writer_engine)'
    negative_pattern: '(import|#)'
    description: "admin_engine/writer_engine used — verify if SELECT (should use reader_engine)"
    convention: "Engine selection"
    notes: "Needs LLM triage: write operations (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE) correctly use writer_engine"
    used_by: [codebase-review]

  - id: missing-writer-engine-guard
    name: "Missing writer_engine null guard"
    category: correctness
    severity: critical
    paths: ["<api module>/"]
    include: ["*.py"]
    exclude: ["*test*"]
    pattern: 'writer_engine\.(begin|connect)\(\)'
    description: "writer_engine.begin()/connect() call — verify enclosing function has 'if not db_config.writer_engine:' guard at entry"
    convention: "Engine null guards"
    notes: "Needs LLM triage: verify each call site's enclosing function starts with 'if not db_config.writer_engine: logger.warning(...); return <safe default>'. Bugs in this category tend to be silent failures — a missing guard typically only surfaces when DB_PASS_WRITER is unset (e.g., dev environments without write credentials), so the check is most valuable as a deterministic preventive."
    used_by: [codebase-review]

  # ── Semgrep suppression hygiene ────────────────────────────────

  - id: nosemgrep-sqlalchemy-pair
    name: "nosemgrep names only one sqlalchemy sibling rule"
    category: correctness
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<api module>/", "<scripts dir>/", "<datajobs dir>/"]
    include: ["*.py"]
    exclude: ["*test*"]
    pattern: 'nosemgrep:.*(sqlalchemy-execute-raw-query|avoid-sqlalchemy-text)'
    negative_pattern: 'sqlalchemy-execute-raw-query.*avoid-sqlalchemy-text|avoid-sqlalchemy-text.*sqlalchemy-execute-raw-query'
    description: "nosemgrep suppression for sqlalchemy.text(variable) names only one of the two sibling rules. Both fire on the same pattern; suppressing one lets the other block every future edit. Name BOTH on the same line: # nosemgrep: python.sqlalchemy.security.sqlalchemy-execute-raw-query,python.sqlalchemy.security.audit.avoid-sqlalchemy-text.avoid-sqlalchemy-text"
    convention: "Semgrep nosemgrep rule IDs"
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: false

  # ── Semgrep / AST diagnostics ──────────────────────────────────
  # These entries have no `pattern:` field. Findings are populated
  # by `_run_semgrep()` in sysop/scripts/run_checks/semgrep.py, which scans
  # the pack's semgrep/*.yaml rules. All start `blocking: false` —
  # CI does not install semgrep yet.

  - id: semgrep-missing-writer-engine-guard
    name: "Missing writer_engine null-guard (semgrep)"
    category: correctness
    severity: medium
    paths: ["<api module>/", "<scripts dir>/", "<datajobs dir>/"]
    description: >
      writer_engine.begin()/.connect() called in a function without
      an 'if not writer_engine:' null-guard at entry
    convention: "Engine null guards"
    used_by: [codebase-review]
    blocking: false

  - id: semgrep-sql-fstring
    name: "F-string SQL in execute() (semgrep)"
    category: security
    severity: high
    paths: ["<api module>/", "<scripts dir>/", "<datajobs dir>/"]
    description: "f-string SQL passed to conn.execute() or sqlalchemy.text()"
    convention: "SQL injection prevention"
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: false

  - id: semgrep-sql-fstring-multiline
    name: "Multi-line f-string SQL, any destination (semgrep)"
    category: security
    severity: high
    paths: ["<api module>/", "<scripts dir>/", "<datajobs dir>/"]
    description: "A triple-quoted f-string whose first content line is a SQL DML keyword. Build the statement with bound parameters instead: conn.execute(sqlalchemy.text(\"... :param ...\"), {\"param\": val})."
    convention: "SQL injection prevention"
    notes: |
      Closes `Q-244` (Phase 217) — the residual that the grep check
      `sql-fstring` names in its own `notes:` and that `semgrep-sql-fstring`
      does not reach. The grep runner is line-oriented, so the f-string prefix
      and the SQL keyword being on different lines makes the shape invisible to
      it; `semgrep-sql-fstring` sees multi-line content but only where the
      f-string is handed STRAIGHT to `.execute()` / `sqlalchemy.text()`.
      Between them, an f-string built on one line and executed later was
      covered by nothing.

      Matches the literal, not its destination, and that is load-bearing: the
      two real production sites in the extraction source are different shapes —
      one binds to a variable, one is returned directly — and the filing
      described only the first. A rule keyed to `$VAR = f"..."` would have
      missed half the measured population.

      Deliberately does NOT re-report the same-line form; that is the grep
      check's job and double-reporting one statement as two findings would make
      the baseline harder to read, not safer.

      Verified against the extraction source's production tree: exactly the 2
      known sites and no others, with 0 false positives across every non-test
      module in this check's declared scope. Fixtures
      `semgrep/fixtures/sql_fstring_{positive,negative}.py` carry both real
      shapes plus the traps: English prose beginning "Deleted", a plain
      docstring, and a keyword that is not the first token on its line.

      `blocking: false` for the same reason as its sibling — a consumer's
      required CI job installs no semgrep binary, so a blocking semgrep check
      would record `skipped: tool-missing` and gate nothing.
    used_by: [codebase-review, security-audit]
    blocking: false
